Introduction
The stock market is a notoriously unpredictable and chaotic environment. For this reason, many mathematical models are employed to attempt to evaluate levels of risk and gauge expectations about the future. In particular, options contracts utilize the Black-Scholes model to set prices (European options contracts, American contracts use a different model).
Overview of Options
Options contracts are contracts that grant the owner permission to buy or sell a stock at a specific price at a specific time. For our report we have analyzed call options specifically, where contracts grant the right to buy a stock. To provide an example, one might buy a call option contract for $30 that grants them the right to buy shares of a stock at $200 at a future date. If the price of the stock on that given date is over $200 the owner will execute their contract, buying shares and instantly selling them for a profit (which may or may not cover the original cost of the contract). Otherwise, they will simply let the contract expire and their loss will be the original cost of it.
The Black-Scholes Model
The Black-Scholes model uses a concept from the field of Physics called Brownian motion to emulate stock price movements. Essentially, a random walk of stock price movements is taken, where stock prices move randomly in a certain direction over daily increments. This randomness comes from a standard normal distribution, and the specific price movements are based on past price dynamics of the given stock being evaluated. Instead of measuring these random walks one by one, Black-Scholes uses differential equations to solve for the expected value of all these walks mathematically. The final prices at the end of each walk can be thought of as being averaged to arrive at a predicted final price for a stock, with all possible price paths being accounted for.
With this, the price of a call option contract is set to be the expected value of the stock minus the price at which it can be bought as specified by the contract (called the strike price), both discounted to their present value at the time of purchase. To discount to present value is to account for the fact that money today can be invested for a future return, so it is essentially more valuable. Discounting adjusts future dollars to what their value would be today. The price of a contract ultimately represents the exact expected value of holding the contract, set efficiently such that no one party can instantly profit from owning it.
 
