The Monte Carlo Approach
While the Black-Scholes model is accepted as the industry standard for European options pricing, there are other possible methods. Where Black-Scholes takes a theoretical expectation of all possible price paths, these price paths can also be manually simulated. In theory, these simulations would converge to the Black-Scholes calculated price with a sufficiently large number of samples. Our approach involves running these simulations and observing the convergence to the Black-Scholes model.
Black Swan Events
One of the flaws of the many mathematical models and theoretical approaches to predicting future performance in the stock market is the inability to respond to real-world events. Because large-scale geopolitical or social events are inherently chaotic and unpredictable, they are rarely reflected in the models, which rather rely on underlying patterns of the market. To explore this potential deficiency, we can adapt our model to incorporate Black Swan events: highly rare and spontaneous real-world events that have major market implications. Black Swan events in history have included the COVID pandemic, the 2008 financial crisis, and the early 2000s dot com bubble, all of which resulted in major price changes across the stock market.
