Black Swan Events
To create a new model incorporating black swan events we will define a new function identical to the original but with some slight modifications. The core premise of this new model is to generate price paths in the same way as before, but add a new term to the formula called “Swing”. Swing has a value of 1 most of the time, but occasionally will alter the price change on a given day of the simulation. The magnitude and frequency of a “Swing” can be modified as parameters in our new function, with defaults being 1 swing per year and randomly either a 10% increase or decrease in price for each swing. Swing reflects black swan events because they theoretically happen as complete shocks to the market with no prior knowledge and have major price implications.
 
  
freq and delta are new parameters in our function representing, respectively, the probability on a given day of a swing occurring and the magnitude of that swing (e.g. 0.1 for 10%).
To accomplish this in the code, we use random number generation and compare it to our predefined frequency of a swing occurring. With this we will have a list of days in each simulation for which a black swan event will occur. We randomly decide whether that swing will be positive or negative and encode it as our swing multiplier (e.g. 0.9 for a 10% decrease in price and 1.1 for a 10% increase, with most values being 1 for no swing occurring).
 
Finally, we duplicate the formula from our original function and multiply it by the swing multiplier to generate an array of price paths that now includes black swan events. Final prices and payoffs are calculated identically to the original function.
 
